    </div>
</section>

<!-- ========== 6. OUTLOOK & CONCLUSIONS ========== -->
<section id="outlook-and-conclusions">
    <h2>6. Outlook &amp; Conclusions</h2>

    <h3>Recent Trends (2020s)</h3>
    <p>The 2020s have been marked by exceptional volatility. The COVID-19 demand destruction of April 2020 (${df[df['Date']==pd.Timestamp('2020-04-01')]['Crude_Oil_Price'].values[0]:.2f}/bbl) was followed by the fastest recovery in oil market history, driven by coordinated OPEC+ cuts and stimulus-fueled demand. The Russia-Ukraine conflict triggered a second spike to $112+ in March 2022.</p>

    <p>Since mid-2022, prices have trended downward, settling in the $60–$90 range. The most recent data shows prices at <strong>${kpis['last_price']:.2f}/bbl</strong> as of {kpis['last_date'].strftime('%B %Y')}, with a March 2026 reading of $87.84 suggesting continued sensitivity to supply disruptions.</p>

    <h3>Key Takeaways</h3>
    <ul style="margin-left:20px; margin-bottom:12px;">
        <li><strong>Structural uptrend persists:</strong> Despite periodic crashes, the CAGR of {kpis['cagr']:.2f}% over {kpis['years']:.0f} years demonstrates a persistent upward bias in nominal oil prices.</li>
        <li><strong>Volatility is the norm, not the exception:</strong> With a coefficient of variation of {(kpis['std_dev']/kpis['mean_price'])*100:.1f}%, oil price volatility significantly exceeds that of most financial assets. The standard deviation of ${kpis['std_dev']:.2f} represents {(kpis['std_dev']/kpis['mean_price'])*100:.1f}% of the mean price.</li>
        <li><strong>Geopolitics remain the dominant driver:</strong> Every major price shock in the dataset — the 1973 embargo, 1979 revolution, 1990 Gulf War, 2008 crisis, 2020 pandemic, 2022 Russia-Ukraine war — originated from geopolitical or macroeconomic disruptions, not gradual supply-demand shifts.</li>
        <li><strong>Regime changes are real and persistent:</strong> Oil prices do not mean-revert to a fixed level. Each decade has operated in a distinct price regime, making historical averages unreliable predictors of future levels.</li>
        <li><strong>The {kpis['highest_decade']} era may be an outlier:</strong> The highest-ever average decade price (${kpis['highest_decade_val']:.2f}/bbl) was sustained by unique confluences of emerging-market demand, supply constraints, and geopolitical risk. Current levels are materially below that peak.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout callout-warning">
